# T5 — capability inventory as a plain user (uid 1000).
# Every row is a command that was actually run; the right column is what
# happened, not what the documentation claims.

## A. expected to work unprivileged
  read /proc/self/stat                           ALLOWED  (unprivileged)
  read /proc/self/status                         ALLOWED  (unprivileged)
  list /proc for all pids                        ALLOWED  (unprivileged)
  read another uid's /proc/1/stat (partial ok)   ALLOWED  (unprivileged)
  read /proc/1/cmdline (root, same-ns)           ALLOWED  (unprivileged)
  inotify watch on an owned directory            ALLOWED  (CREATE observed)
  AF_UNIX socket bind+connect+reply              ALLOWED  (socket 600)
  sqlite write in an owned directory             ALLOWED  (sum=42 journal_mode=delete)
  hardlink + rename in an owned dir              ALLOWED  (unprivileged)

## B. expected to require privilege
  read /etc/shadow                               DENIED   (needs privilege)
  list /root                                     DENIED   (needs privilege)
  read /proc/1/environ (root process)            DENIED   (needs privilege)
  read /proc/1/stack                             DENIED   (needs privilege)
  read /proc/1/mem                               DENIED   (needs privilege)
  read /proc/1/root                              DENIED   (needs privilege)
  inotify watch on /root                         DENIED   (needs privilege)
  inotify watch on /var/lib/private              DENIED   (needs privilege)
  btrfs subvolume snapshot /                     DENIED   (needs privilege)
  mount -t tmpfs                                 DENIED   (needs privilege)
  umount                                         DENIED   (needs privilege)

## C. NO_NEW_PRIVS suppresses a setuid gain (read-only probe)
# /usr/bin/passwd is setuid root; 'passwd -S <user>' only READS the
# status field. Nothing is modified. It is used solely to observe
# whether a setuid bit is still honoured under PR_SET_NO_NEW_PRIVS.
$ passwd -S USER
  USER P 2026-03-01 -1 99999 7 -1
$ setpriv --no-new-privs passwd -S USER
  USER P
# The second run cannot read the shadow status field: the setuid gain was
# suppressed by the kernel, not refused by the file mode.
